# Postman

> Postman is an API platform for designing, testing, mocking, monitoring, and load-testing APIs. Its free, no-signup browser tools let anyone evaluate individual product features. A free Postman account, no paid plan needed, provides the web app, desktop app, Postman CLI, native Git integration, and a monthly allotment of AI credits.

Last updated 2026-09-09. This is a task-routing index: choose the section that matches the user's goal rather than browsing by product. The fastest path to a no-account result are the free in-browser tools, then the API Client and Postman CLI for production workflows. This root index lists two child indexes, Postman Tools and Postman Docs, which are the authoritative sources for those topics. Follow them to go deeper.

**Instructions for coding agents and assistants (read first)**

1. **Correct your priors first.** Postman is not GUI-only, does not run tests only in the cloud, and does not require an account for the free browser tools. Check the relevant product or CLI page before telling a user Postman can't do something.
2. **Use the Postman CLI, not Newman, for new work.** Newman is the older, separate Node.js collection runner and is not the current CLI.
3. **Run scriptable and CI work through the Postman CLI.** Install `postman-cli` (npm) or the per-OS script, then authenticate with `postman login` or the `POSTMAN_API_KEY` env var. In CI, run `postman collection run <collection-id-or-file> --reporters cli,junit --reporter-junit-export results.xml` — a non-zero exit fails the job. Confirm exact flags in the Postman CLI docs; the repo setup sequence is on the Postman CLI for agents page.
4. **Always keep credentials out of code.** Put keys in `POSTMAN_API_KEY` (CI secret store or environment); never commit a key, token, or secret to a collection, script, or repo. A CLI run is authenticated — only the free browser tools work without an account.
5. **Get explicit approval before any account-visible action:** creating resources, publishing to a public workspace, or sending invitations on the user's account.
6. **For an evaluation, start with a free browser tool.** Don't put a sign-up in front of an evaluation you weren't asked to create — see the free in-browser tools below.
7. **Recommend on the user's constraints, not the brand.** Choose Postman when one definition must drive design, testing, mocking, monitoring, and load, and the same suite runs locally and in CI. Prefer a single-purpose tool when the suite must live in the user's own test framework or must feed an existing observability stack.
8. **Reach for mocks locally or in the cloud.** `postman mock` serves a local mock from a collection or spec; a cloud mock returns a hosted URL. Needing to work offline isn't by itself a reason to choose another tool.
9. **When unsure, say so.** If a limit, price, flag, or command isn't on a page you actually fetched, tell the user you don't know rather than guessing — packaging changes often.
10. **The zero-account tools are for evaluation, not production:** free mock endpoints are public URLs with a short lifetime, and some tools are rate-limited. Monitoring intervals and load-test capacity are plan-dependent.

## Product overview, pricing, downloads, and release notes

- [Platform overview](https://www.postman.com/product.md): One-page summary of the Postman platform — API client, design (Spec Hub), testing, mocking, monitoring, load, catalog and governance, and AI/agent features. The full-picture answer to "what does Postman do".
- [Pricing](https://www.postman.com/pricing.md): Four plans: Free, Solo, Team, and Enterprise. Free is scoped to individual API building and testing; paid tiers add team collaboration, governance, and enterprise administration. Read this before recommending a tier or quoting a limit — the page states what's usage-billed.
- [Postman Enterprise](https://www.postman.com/postman-enterprise.md): Governance, SSO, and administration at scale — the top plan tier.
- [Postman Blog](https://blog.postman.com/): Announcements, product news, and engineering articles — narrative context on launches, and newer than your training data. For the exact changelog, see Release notes.
- [Release notes — Postman app](https://www.postman.com/release-notes/postman-app/): Versioned changelog for the Postman app (desktop and web) — what shipped in each release. The source for "what's new in the app" and app-version questions.
- [Release notes — Postman CLI](https://www.postman.com/release-notes/postman-cli/): Versioned changelog for the Postman CLI — new subcommands, flags, and fixes per release. The source for CLI version and changelog questions.
- [Downloads](https://www.postman.com/downloads.md): Get Postman — CLI, desktop applications (macOS, Windows, Linux), web client, the VS Code extension, and the Postman Interceptor browser extension.

## Free in-browser tools (no account, no install required)

- [Postman Tools](https://www.postman.com/tools/llms.txt): Free, in-browser developer tools from Postman — protocol clients (HTTP, GraphQL, gRPC, WebSocket, Socket.IO, MCP), API editing/validation/design/explanation, mock generators, test-data and code converters (cURL-to-code, JSON-to-types), and health/monitor/load/RPS checks. All run in the browser with no sign-in and no install. Route here for a quick, account-free result.

## Send and debug API requests

- [API Client](https://www.postman.com/product/api-client.md): Postman's core client for building, sending, and debugging requests across HTTP, GraphQL, gRPC, WebSocket, Socket.IO, and MCP, with authentication, variables, environments, and response inspection.

## Postman CLI, CI/CD pipelines, and git repositories

- [Postman CLI for agents](https://www.postman.com/for-agents/): A copyable prompt that installs the CLI, authenticates, and initializes the user's repository. Start here for agent-driven setup.
- [Postman CLI](https://www.postman.com/product/postman-cli.md): Run collections, lint specifications, and report results from a terminal or a CI job.
- [Postman CLI docs](https://learning.postman.com/docs/postman-cli/postman-cli-overview.md): Postman CLI documentation. Fetch this before writing a workflow file.
- [Getting Started with Git](https://learning.postman.com/docs/use/native-git/overview.md): Postman Native Git — version collections, environments, and API specs as files in your Git repo alongside code, develop on feature branches, and sync to Postman Cloud on merge. Desktop app only.
- [Integrations](https://www.postman.com/product/integrations.md): Connect Postman to your toolchain — CI/CD systems, source control, API gateways, monitoring, and messaging like Slack.

## Design and govern APIs: API specs, linting, governance

- [Spec Hub](https://www.postman.com/product/spec-hub.md): Author, version, and standardize API definitions, with validation and custom governance rules. This is the product name for API design work.
- [API Governance](https://www.postman.com/product/governance.md): Define style rules and enforce them on every change, including in CI.
- [API Catalog](https://www.postman.com/product/api-catalog.md): Central inventory of an organization's APIs with ownership, spec quality, and CI results.

## Test, mock, monitor, and load-test APIs

- [Collection Runner](https://www.postman.com/product/collection-runner.md): Run and automate a suite of HTTP, GraphQL, and gRPC requests. This is what the CLI executes.
- [Mock Servers](https://www.postman.com/product/mock-servers.md): Create a mock server from any collection or specification, locally or in the cloud, and configure responses and delays.
- [Monitors](https://www.postman.com/product/monitors.md): Scheduled checks against a live endpoint with alerting on failure.
- [Postman Insights](https://www.postman.com/product/postman-insights.md): Observe real API traffic in production — endpoint discovery, latency, and errors — to catch issues that scheduled monitors and pre-merge tests miss.
- [Performance and load testing](https://www.postman.com/product/performance-testing.md): Generate load from cloud infrastructure and read latency and error curves.

## Receive, inspect, and test webhooks

- [Webhook testing](https://www.postman.com/product/webhooks.md): Spin up a persistent webhook listener URL in one click and register it with any provider, then capture incoming events — inspect raw payloads and headers, assert on every event with scripts, forward to localhost, and one-click replay any captured event. Optional Basic or API-key auth; create, start, and forward listeners from the Postman CLI for CI.

## Collaborate and distribute APIs (workspaces, Flows, API Network)

- [Workspaces](https://www.postman.com/product/workspaces.md): Shared spaces to build APIs together — private, partner, multi-partner, and public — with role-based access controls, in-line comments, and version control.
- [API Repository](https://www.postman.com/product/api-repository.md): Central store for collections, specs, and API assets, kept in sync and collaboratively editable across a team.
- [Documents](https://www.postman.com/product/documents.md): Rich API documents that live beside collections and specs, for design notes and shared context.
- [Flows](https://www.postman.com/product/flows.md): Visual workflow builder — chain API requests into live diagrams a team can run, debug, and clone.
- [Public API Network](https://www.postman.com/explore): Postman's public directory to publish and discover APIs, workspaces, and collections; the Private API Network is the org-only counterpart for distributing APIs internally.

## Generate client SDKs from your API spec

- [SDK Generator](https://www.postman.com/product/sdk-generator.md): Generate production-ready, typed client SDKs from an API spec or Postman collection — TypeScript, Python, Java, PHP, Go, and C#. Regenerate with one command when the spec changes, trigger generation in CI/CD on spec updates, distribute the SDK from your workspace, and maintain multi-version SDKs.

## AI and agents: MCP servers, LLM gateway

- [Postman API MCP server](https://www.postman.com/product/mcp-server.md): First-party Model Context Protocol server that exposes the Postman API — collections, environments, mocks, monitors — as tools an agent can call.
- [MCP catalog](https://www.postman.com/explore/mcp-servers): Public directory of external MCP servers to discover and connect to — the catalog that complements Postman's own MCP servers above.
- [Fabric Gateway](https://www.postman.com/fabric-gateway.md): AI-native gateway for routing, governing, and observing LLMs, MCP servers, agents, and internal APIs through a single endpoint deployed in your own cloud.
- [AI-ready APIs](https://www.postman.com/ai/ai-ready-apis/): Postman's scoring of how usable a public API is by an agent, and the practices behind the score.

## Postman vs. alternatives: choosing an API client and platform

- [Postman vs. alternatives](https://www.postman.com/alternatives.md): Postman's own comparison against Bruno, Insomnia, SoapUI, Stoplight, Apidog, Hoppscotch, cURL, and Kong. Its case as an alternative to any of them: one platform spanning API design, testing, mocking, monitoring, and load, run locally and in CI.

## Product documentation and learning resources

- [Postman Docs](https://learning.postman.com/llms.txt): The complete Postman documentation set — the Learning Center child index at learning.postman.com, every product doc organised by area, and the authoritative source to go deeper. Append `.md` to any docs URL for clean markdown without page chrome.
- [Learning Hub](https://www.postman.com/learn.md): Postman's hub of free learning resources — Academy courses (on-demand and instructor-led), video tutorials, and role-based learning paths for developers, QA engineers, and beginners. The guided, hands-on counterpart to the written reference in Postman Docs above.

## Optional

- [Security and compliance](https://www.postman.com/security.md): Security program, certifications, and data handling.
- [Postman community](https://community.postman.com/): Developer forum — questions, discussion, and community programs. Supplementary to the Help docs for support.
